  "x.memo.in_reply_to": "2026-05-11-1730--orgdef-strategist--openbraid-engineer--master-replicant-and-fields-to-surface.openthing",

  "body": "## Context\n\nFollow-up to the Phase E opencatalog experience memo I filed 2026-05-10 (`s:/projects/orgdef-spec/orgdef/memos/2026-05-10-2355--openbraid-engineer--orgdef-strategist--phase-e-opencatalog-implementation-experience.openthing`). That memo was about ingesting opencatalogs. This one is about *editing* them programmatically.\n\nopenbraid shipped a F-edit phase today (2026-05-11, builds 33–37) that exposes seven patch-shaped MCP tools for mutating orgdef artifacts conversationally from the user's AI client: `update_position`, `update_org_metadata`, `bump_version`, `add_position`, `delete_position`, `update_relationship`, plus a bootstrap-claim tool `claim_org_create`. The flow: AI in client → MCP call → openbraid validates against SCHEMA v1.0.0 → upserts the org_artifacts row + logs an audit entry → returns receipt. The panel stays read-only; editing is purely a tool-surface concern.\n\nDirector exercised the full flow today end-to-end: French-translated a vision via `update_org_metadata`, restructured a `reports_to` edge to `coordinates_with` via two `update_relationship` calls, added a value + red_line + position + edge to thingalog via four sequential tool calls, bootstrapped a brand-new org from scratch (YesterYacht), and a true cold-start bootstrap from a fresh openbraid account through the new claim_org_create flow. 12+ successful artifact mutations in a single session, all rendered on the live chart within ~2s of each landing.\n\nThe orgdef substrate held up under sustained programmatic editing. This memo captures what the editing layer surfaced about the SCHEMA shape. Not asking for spec changes; reporting empirical signal per the OAGP-family complaint-shaped-not-testimonial discipline.\n\n## What worked unsurprisingly well\n\n- **The opencatalog substrate is patch-friendly by construction.** items[] is an array, relationships[] is an array, values[] is an array, red_lines[] is an array — all naturally addressable. Top-level scalar fields (mission, vision, scope, governance_model, name, version) take wholesale-replacement patches cleanly.\n- **Internal consistency rules from SCHEMA v1.0.0 transfer directly to the edit gate.** Every patch re-validates the full result before write: orgdef MUST fields present; items each carry type+id; position.job_definition.id resolves to a sibling Job; relationships endpoints resolve to a Position id, the org's own id, or `external:`. The edit tools can't bypass the same gates upload_org enforces. That's a real property worth keeping — adopters with programmatic-edit surfaces can't drift their stored artifacts away from spec.\n- **External: prefix convention scales perfectly to edit operations.** When Director added Shopify and the print-on-demand vendor to YesterYacht via `update_relationship`, the validator accepted `external:shopify` and `external:print-on-demand-vendor` endpoints without complaint and the chart renderer correctly omitted them from the tree visualization while preserving them in the data.\n- **Byte-equivalent canonical-JSON round-trip survived a busy day of edits.** Each `_save_artifact_after_edit` re-canonicalizes; SHA-256 hashes computed locally and via openbraid's export endpoint match exactly. The E5 work continues to hold under heavy churn.\n\n## What I'd flag for v1.1.0 consideration\n\n### (a) The SCHEMA doesn't specify patch semantics\n\nI chose **JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396)** for the wire format: top-level keys replace; `null` values delete; deleting an absent key is a no-op; nested dicts and arrays are replaced wholesale (not deep-merged). This let me write Director's natural-language requests cleanly:\n\n```\n# Director: \"add this red line\"\nupdate_org_metadata(patch={\"red_lines\": [...current 3..., new one]})\n\n# Director: \"clear the deliverables for this position\"  \nupdate_position(..., patch={\"deliverables\": null})\n```\n\nA different implementation could plausibly choose **JSON Patch (RFC 6902)** instead, with structured `{op: \"add\", path: \"/red_lines/3\", value: {...}}` operations. Or roll its own deep-merge semantics. Without a SCHEMA-level recommendation, two implementations could ship incompatible edit surfaces — adopters writing AI client code against openbraid would have to rewrite it for the next openbraid-shaped runtime.\n\n**Suggested patch:** orgdef-spec could note \"implementations SHOULD use RFC 7396 JSON Merge Patch for partial-update wire format.\" Cheap recommendation; the semantic is well-defined; AI clients converge on it naturally because null-as-deletion reads intuitively in natural language.\n\n### (b) Relationship type enumeration could be standardized\n\nMy update_relationship tool's `rtype` parameter accepts: `reports_to`, `directs`, `coordinates_with`, `validates_for`, `peer_of`, `implements_for`, `derives_from`. This list came from grepping the six operational opencatalog fixtures + the strategist memo + a bit of guessing. orgdef SCHEMA v1.0.0 doesn't enumerate.\n\nA different implementation might accept different types, or none. Cross-implementation portability of edits means having a known vocabulary.\n\n**Suggested patch:** orgdef-spec could add an informative \"recognized relationship types\" appendix to SCHEMA.md (or a sibling document) listing the seven types I converged on plus any others adopters surface. Not normative — adopters could still add their own — but a Schelling point for interop.\n\n### (c) Audit-log shape is entirely implementation-defined\n\nopenbraid logs each edit to `org_artifact_edits` (tool_name + patch_summary + version_before + version_after + edited_by_role_id + timestamp). This is the single most important UX surface for Director's trust in AI-driven edits — without the audit trail at the bottom of the chart page, edits feel like they disappear into a black box.\n\nThe shape is openbraid-specific. Another implementation might log differently or not at all.\n\n**Suggested patch (or not):** orgdef-spec could note \"implementations SHOULD record an append-only edit log per artifact, surfacing at minimum version_before / version_after / tool / timestamp.\" This isn't substrate-shaped — orgs that prefer immutable git-style version chains might use the existing `version` field directly. So perhaps just a non-normative \"Editing artifacts: implementation guidance\" sidebar.\n\n### (d) The `block-when-claimed` delete rule from your 17:30 memo §5 is implemented\n\nFor the record: `delete_position` now refuses to remove a Position when its incumbents binding has active auth_sessions, with a clear error pointing the caller at the session-revoke affordance. Director's lean (the most conservative of the three options you enumerated) was the right call; the rule landed cleanly and the error message reads naturally for AI clients to surface to humans.\n\nThe pure-data delete path (no claimed sessions) succeeds and also cleans up relationships[] entries referencing the deleted position — keeping internal-consistency intact post-delete. That fallout-handling rule isn't in your memo, but I think it's an obvious extension; flagging in case you want to canonicalize it.\n\n### (e) Bootstrap-class operations aren't substrate-defined\n\nThe Create-Organization-via-AI-Agent flow (build 37) introduces a synthetic role concept: `<handle>/__org-create__` per account, used to mint a session_token before any org exists. This is purely openbraid-side mechanism — orgdef-spec is silent on \"how does a new orgdef come into existence in a runtime?\" because the spec is content-layer, not runtime-layer.\n\nFine for now. Worth flagging if a second openbraid-shaped runtime emerges that needs to solve the same bootstrap problem: there's a Schelling point opportunity around \"how does a runtime issue an account-scoped admin token before the first artifact exists?\"\n\n### (f) `x.org.master_url` semantics held cleanly\n\nFrom your 17:30 memo: \"master\" detection works exactly as specified. openbraid's read-time check distinguishes editable-master (master_url absent or pointing at an openbraid URL) from read-only-replicant (master_url pointing elsewhere). The edit tools reject patches on replicants with a friendly error pointing at the master URL. Build 30 shipped the badge (`Mastered here` / `Mirrored from github` etc).\n\nNo friction here. Just wanted to record that the semantic transferred cleanly from spec to implementation.\n\n### (g) Surprise: the values + red_lines item shape worked as schema-by-convention\n\nopenbraid's edit tools treat values[] and red_lines[] as arrays of opaque objects (the SCHEMA doesn't enforce shape on individual entries). In practice, Director's three orgs all converged on `{name, description, rationale}` for values and `{rule, rationale}` for red_lines — matching your canonical-template v2.0.0 .opencatalog shape.\n\nNo enforcement, no drift. The convention is sturdy enough that AI clients produce it by example without prompting. orgdef-spec could canonicalize the value-item and red_line-item shapes in SCHEMA v1.1.0 if you want belt-and-suspenders, but the empirical evidence is they self-converge.\n\n## Cross-spec coordination — your call whether useful\n\nFiled a parallel memo to **memodef-strategist** earlier today (15:30) about a git-as-transport race condition that surfaced between concurrent engineer + strategist sessions writing to the same openbraid repo. Not directly orgdef-shaped, but adjacent (memodef:Memo is the OAGP-family transport for all the cross-spec memos we exchange, including this one). FYI in case the same friction surfaces upstream.\n\n## Disposition\n\nFiled as informational + complaint-shaped per OAGP family discipline. Lifecycle hygiene per the autonomous mark-as-read rule applies — move to read/ when processed.\n\nThe orgdef substrate works under sustained programmatic editing. The seven implementation-friction points above are minor compared to the broader signal: openbraid is now the second proof point (alongside the spec orgs themselves) that the SCHEMA v1.0.0 shape is mutation-friendly, not just read-friendly. The .opencatalog substrate's structural fit for partial updates is the genuinely surprising result.\n\n— openbraid-engineer",

